<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Saybrook</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old Saybrook is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a>. It grew from Saybrook Fort, built in 1635–1636 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop-the-younger">John Winthrop the Younger</a> for the Puritan holders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick-patent">Warwick Patent</a> and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fiennes-1st-viscount-saye-and-sele">Lord Saye and Sele</a> and Lord Brooke. The settlement joined the Connecticut Colony in 1644, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale College</a> was based there before moving to New Haven in 1716.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-saybrook.jpg" alt="Old Saybrook" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juliejules · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saybrook_breakwater_light.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Old Saybrook lies in Middlesex County on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a>, where it flows into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a>. It covers 56.0 square kilometres, about 30 per cent of it water, and is joined to Old Lyme on the east bank by the Raymond E. Baldwin Bridge, which carries Interstate 95. Its population was 10,481 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> settlers set up a short-lived trading post here in 1624, Kievits Hoek, or Plover's Corner. The Saybrook Colony was founded in late 1635 by a group claiming the land by a deed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-rich-2nd-earl-of-warwick">Robert Rich, 2nd Earl of Warwick</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop-the-younger">John Winthrop the Younger</a> as governor, aided by Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-fenwick">George Fenwick</a> and Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gardiner">Lion Gardiner</a>. It struggled because its investors, supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a>, stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, and in 1644 Fenwick agreed to merge it with the Connecticut Colony, whose settlers bought the land and fort. The seal Fenwick brought from England gave the design of the Flag of Connecticut.</p><p>Pequots besieged the fort from September 1636 to March 1637, after Massachusetts Bay attacked Manisses, Pequot and Western Niantic villages. They killed and wounded more than 20 settlers over eight months, and the siege escalated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a>. In 1659 Major John Mason, who had commanded the fort from 1647, led most of the settlers to found Norwich. The Collegiate School of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> was chartered at Old Saybrook on 9 October 1701 and moved to New Haven in 1716, becoming Yale. The town was created from Saybrook in 1852, and in 1854 its northern part became Essex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The former town hall of 1901, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> until the 1940s, reopened in 2009 as the Katharine Hepburn Cultural Arts Center; the actress lived in the borough of Fenwick. The Lynde Point and Saybrook Breakwater lighthouses are on the National Register of Historic Places, and the train station, opened in 1873, has Amtrak service.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Old Saybrook, Connecticut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
